'''vivarium''' (vī-vā′ri-um), ''n.''; pl. ''vivariums, vivaria'' (-umz, -ä). [< L. ''vivarium'', an inclosure in which game, fish, etc., are kept alive, < ''vivus'', living, alive, < ''vivere'', live: see ''vivid''.] A place where animals of any kind are kept alive in their natural state as far as possible; a vivary; a zoölogical park. {{smaller|A vivarium may be adapted to all kinds of animals; one for special purposes may be called by a particular name. A place for fish, etc., is an ''aquarium'' (of which the generic opposite is ''terrarium''); for birds, an ''aviary''; for frogs, a ''ranarium''; for mollusks, a ''snailery'', etc. A vivarium in popular language takes its name from the animals kept in it, as ''piggery'', ''hennery'', etc.}}
{{smaller block|There is also adjoining to it a ''vivarium'' for estriges, peacocks, swanns, cranes, etc. ''Evelyn'', Diary, Nov. 17, 1644.}}

'''vivary''' (vī′va-ri), ''n.''; pl. ''vivaries'' (-riz). [< L. ''vivarium'': see ''vivarium''.] A vivarium. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|The garden has every variety, hills, dales, rocks, grooves, aviaries, ''vivaries'', fountaines. ''Evelyn'', Diary, Oct. 22, 1644.
<poem>That cage and ''vivary''
Of fowls and beasts.</poem>
{{right|''Donne'', Progress of the Soul, iii.}}}}

'''vivat''' (vī′vat), ''n.'' [= F. ''vivat'' (as L.), also ''vive'' = It. Sp. Pg. ''viva''; < L. ''vivat'', 3d pers. sing. pres. subj. of ''vivere'', live: see ''vivid''. Cf. ''viva'', ''vive''<sup>2</sup>.] An exclamation of applause or joy; a viva.
{{smaller block|Twenty-seven millions travelling on such courses, with gold jingling in every pocket, with ''vivats'' heaven high, are incessantly advancing . . . to the firm land’s end.
{{right|''Carlyle.''}}}}

