'''viveret†''', ''n.'' [ME., < OF. ''vivier'', < L. ''vivarium'', a vivarium: see ''vivarium''.] A vivarium.

{{smaller block|And before the Mynstre of this Ydole is a ''Vyvere'', in maner of a gret Lake fulle of Watre: and there in Pilgrymes casten Gold and Sylver, Perles and precyous Stones, with outen nombre, in stede of Offrynges.
{{right|''Mandeville'', Travels, p. 174.}}}}

'''Viverra''' (vī-ver′ä̤), ''n.'' [NL., < L. ''viverra'', a ferret.] A Linnean genus of carnivorous quadrupeds which contained 6 species (now placed in different modern families), and which has by successive restrictions been confined to the true civets as the type of the family ''Viverridæ''. See cuts under ''civet-cat'' and ''tangalung''.

'''Viverridæ''' (vī-ver′i-dē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''Viverra'' + ''-idæ''.] A family of carnivorous mammals, of the æluroid or feline series of the fissiped ''Feræ'', typified by the genus ''Viverra''. {{smaller|The family has been made to cover a miscellaneous assortment of}}

readings.c3s3:
{{smaller block|these are respectively styled ''æluropod'' or cat-footed, and ''cynopod'' or dog-footed. The former is the viverrine section in strictness, the latter the herpestine section; each has several subfamilies. (''a'') To the viverrine section belong the typical civets and genets, forming the subfamily ''Viverrinæ;'' the prionodons, ''Prionodontinæ;'' the galidians, ''Galidiinæ;'' the palm-cats or paradoxures, with curly tails, ''Paradoxurinæ;'' the binturongs, ''Arctictidinæ;'' the hemigales, ''Hemigalinæ;'' and the cynogales, ''Cynogalinæ.'' (See cuts under ''civet-cat, Cynogale, Galidictis, genet, musang, nandine,'' and ''tangalung.'') (''b'') To the herpestine section belong the numerous ichneumons, mongooses, etc., forming the restricted ''Herpestinæ,'' of which upward of 12 genera and many species are known; the cynictis, ''Cynictidinæ;'' the ''Rhinogalinæ;'' and the suricates, ''Crossarchinæ.'' (See cuts under ''Cynictis, ichneumon,'' and ''Suricata.'') In all, there are some 30 genera of ''Viverridæ,'' of 11 subfamilies of 2 sections. Besides furnishing the civet of commerce, the ''Viverridæ'' take the place of ordinary cats and weasels in destroying smaller vermin, and some of them are of the greatest service, owing to their destruction of venomous reptiles, crocodiles’ eggs, etc.}}

'''viverriform''' (vī-ver′i-fôrm), ''a.'' [< L. ''viverra,'' ferret, + ''forma,'' form.] Viverrine in form and structure: noting the large series of Old World quadrupeds of the families ''Viverridæ'' and ''Eupleridæ.''

'''Viverrinæ''' (viv-e-rī′nē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''Viverra'' + ''-inæ.''] A division of ''Viverridæ.'' {{smaller|(''a'') Broadly, one of two subfamilies of ''Viverridæ,'' the other being ''Herpestinæ,'' distinguishing the civets, genets, etc., from the ichneumons, etc.; the cat-footed ''Viverridæ,'' as distin-}}

